WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.042
Password reset is a high-risk account recovery workflow because it can transfer control of an account when handled poorly.

2
00:00:08.042 --> 00:00:11.057
A reset flow is not just a convenience feature.

3
00:00:11.057 --> 00:00:18.480
It is an alternative path into the account, and it should be designed with the same care as primary authentication.

4
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.207
Reset tokens should be time-limited, purpose-specific, protected, and one-time use where possible.

5
00:00:25.207 --> 00:00:36.419
A token created for password reset should not be reusable for other account actions, should expire within a defined window, and should become invalid after successful use.

6
00:00:36.419 --> 00:00:45.543
The application should also handle repeated requests predictably so users receive help without creating unnecessary operational noise.

7
00:00:45.543 --> 00:00:49.332
Reset links and tokens need logging and analytics review.

8
00:00:49.332 --> 00:00:57.914
Tokens can appear in URLs, emails, browser history, support screenshots, analytics tools, reverse proxy logs, and error reports.

9
00:00:57.914 --> 00:01:01.549
Those systems should redact or avoid sensitive values.

10
00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:07.657
A reset token should never become a long-lived artifact in observability or support tooling.

11
00:01:07.657 --> 00:01:14.461
Password change by an authenticated user and password reset through recovery are different workflows.

12
00:01:14.461 --> 00:01:21.420
A signed-in user changing a known password may require current password verification and session review.

13
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:28.147
A recovery flow may require additional notification, token validation, and stronger audit evidence.

14
00:01:28.147 --> 00:01:33.715
Treating the two paths separately makes policy decisions easier to test and explain.

15
00:01:33.715 --> 00:01:42.452
After a password change or reset, review existing sessions, remembered devices, recovery codes, MFA state, and user notifications.

16
00:01:42.452 --> 00:01:45.777
Some applications invalidate existing sessions.

17
00:01:45.777 --> 00:01:48.097
Others apply risk-based handling.

18
00:01:48.097 --> 00:01:58.690
User notification can help detect unwanted recovery activity, and support-assisted recovery should have clear identity verification and audit expectations.
